From 116897762d4fd8bf1e44b5da01d7a8ba0b4287bd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: arena-agent <arena-agent@local>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 12:04:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Report which setup step is running, and on what hardware
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three reports arrived asking whether setup had failed, and none of them could
answer it: [signer] carried only stage, percent and hardwareID. "stage: setup"
says that something is happening and nothing about which of six steps, for how
long, or on what.

Signer.create() now takes an onStep callback and records the start and end of
machine.open, machine.initialize, certificate.fetch, exchange.1, setup.post and
exchange.2. Each step takes one timestamp, shared by its begin and end events,
so endedAt - at is what that step actually cost. The events travel through the
worker into the store, bounded at 80 so a session that retries several times
still produces a pasteable report.

The report gains stuckInStep and setupTimeline, which separate the two failures
that looked identical: a stall in certificate.fetch or setup.post is the server
reaching Apple, while one in machine.open, machine.initialize or exchange.* is
the device failing to keep up with the emulation.

It also gains a [device] section — cpuCores, deviceMemoryGB, platform,
visibilityState. Setup is emulated x86-64, so its cost tracks the hardware far
more than anything in the deployment, and every published timing for it is a
desktop; without these a phone's report cannot be compared to anything.
visibilityState earns its place separately, because a backgrounded tab can have
its worker suspended, which presents exactly as a hung setup.

Events carry step names only — no URLs, no setup buffer, and the hardware id
stays masked — so the report's opening promise about credentials still holds by
construction.

The timeline test caught a real defect in its first run: the first version
paired each step with a separate end entry, so a step whose end lived on its own
event was reported as STILL RUNNING even though it had finished.

Co-authored-by: arena-agent <297053741+arena-agent@users.noreply.github.com>
